.TH SSH2RSA 1
.SH NAME
ssh2rsa \- convert an OpenSSH private key to Plan 9 RSA key format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/ssh2rsa
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ssh2rsa
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.B auth/ssh2rsa
(from
.BR sys/src/cmd/auth/ssh2rsa.c ,
9front). See
.IR rsa2ssh (1)'s
own DESCRIPTION for why this sits under
.B appl/cmd/auth
rather than
.BR p9/cmd .
.PP
.I Ssh2rsa
reads an RSA private key stored in the binary
.B openssh-key-v1
format - the format
.B ssh-keygen
actually writes, base64-armored between
.B "-----BEGIN OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY-----"
markers - from
.I file
or standard input, and prints a Plan 9
.B "key service=ssh proto=rsa ..."
line, the same key-attribute-line format
.IR rsagen (1)
already emits.
.PP
This command only ever sees the ALREADY-BASE64-DECODED binary blob.
The real pipeline it is meant to sit in, converting an
.B id_rsa
as output by Unix
.BR ssh-keygen :
.IP
.EX
auth/pemdecode 'OPENSSH PRIVATE KEY' id_rsa | auth/ssh2rsa
.EE
.LP
stripping the PEM armor and base64-decoding it is
.IR pemdecode (1)'s
job, not this command's.
.PP
Only a single
.RB ( nkeys==1 ),
unencrypted (
.B none
cipher) RSA key is supported - matching the source exactly. An
encrypted key correctly fails with
.BR "private key seems encrypted" .
.SH SOURCE
.B appl/cmd/auth/ssh2rsa.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/auth/ssh2rsa.c ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ssh2rsatest (1),
.IR rsa2ssh (1),
.IR rsagen (1)
.SH BUGS
No plan9port copy of this file exists, and no prebuilt binary for it
existed on this host either (same situation
.IR urlencode (1)
was first in) - verified instead against a real
.B ssh-keygen
-generated key, cross-checked field-by-field against
.BR "openssl rsa -text" 's
independent decoding of the same key material (see
.IR ssh2rsatest (1) 's
own header comment).
.PP
Real key-component length fields are read as a plain 32-bit
.B int
- like the source's own
.B u32int
.B GET4()
macro, this has no defined behavior once a length exceeds 2^31, but
every real RSA key component is a few hundred bytes at most, so this
is never reachable on real input.
